1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,181
How does the team know if a new valve will
fit?

2
00:00:03,878 --> 00:00:08,533
Before a catheter-based valve replacement,
the Heart Team builds a map of the heart

3
00:00:08,591 --> 00:00:11,877
valve and the vessels that could carry the
device to it.

4
00:00:12,574 --> 00:00:16,753
First, the team reviews the person’s
symptoms and the severity of aortic

5
00:00:16,799 --> 00:00:22,117
stenosis. An echocardiogram shows the
valve opening and how blood moves

6
00:00:22,163 --> 00:00:25,727
through it. It also helps assess the
pumping chamber.

7
00:00:26,168 --> 00:00:30,546
The team uses these findings to decide
whether valve replacement needs to be

8
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:31,393
considered.

9
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:36,200
A CT scan can show the aortic root in
detail.

10
00:00:36,711 --> 00:00:40,960
The team measures the ring where the new
valve would sit and studies nearby

11
00:00:41,029 --> 00:00:44,884
structures, including the openings of the
coronary arteries.

12
00:00:45,395 --> 00:00:48,297
It also checks where calcium is
concentrated.

13
00:00:48,901 --> 00:00:52,500
Those details matter for how a replacement
valve could fit.

14
00:00:53,313 --> 00:00:57,237
The same type of scan can show a potential
route through the arteries.

15
00:00:57,934 --> 00:01:01,742
The most common catheter route is through
an artery in the groin.

16
00:01:02,438 --> 00:01:06,502
The team checks the route’s shape and size
and where there is calcium.

17
00:01:07,013 --> 00:01:11,042
If that route is unsuitable, the team
discusses other options.

18
00:01:11,552 --> 00:01:16,243
The assessment can also include blood
tests, a heart rhythm tracing and a look

19
00:01:16,289 --> 00:01:17,972
at the coronary arteries.

20
00:01:18,669 --> 00:01:22,581
The team considers other health
conditions, day-to-day function and what

21
00:01:22,616 --> 00:01:24,497
the patient wants from treatment.

22
00:01:25,101 --> 00:01:29,153
These details help it weigh the potential
benefits and risks of the available

23
00:01:29,199 --> 00:01:30,070
options.

24
00:01:31,184 --> 00:01:35,480
The Heart Team brings together specialists
in imaging, catheter treatment and

25
00:01:35,573 --> 00:01:40,438
surgery. They review the test results and
discuss which options are

26
00:01:40,473 --> 00:01:45,430
suitable. Sometimes the assessment leads
to further testing or a surgical

27
00:01:45,488 --> 00:01:50,632
opinion. The recommendation takes both the
valve anatomy and the patient’s

28
00:01:50,713 --> 00:01:52,327
wider health into account.

29
00:01:52,931 --> 00:01:57,714
The patient can ask why an option is being
recommended, what its risks are, and what

30
00:01:57,784 --> 00:02:02,892
recovery could involve. The team explains
the proposed next steps.

31
00:02:03,496 --> 00:02:08,511
In the next film, we’ll follow one planned
example to see how a transcatheter valve

32
00:02:08,569 --> 00:02:12,401
reaches the heart and takes over the work
of the narrowed valve.
